Wait a sec — but in the case of "GNU Image Manipulation Program", "GNU" → G, N, U? G is at index 0, N is uppercase but the previous character G is also uppercase → not included. U is the same. Good. "HyperText" → H is at index 0, T is uppercase and the previous character p is lowercase → included. Good.

But watch out for the first character: what if the first character is a non-alphabetic character like "_"? In the case of "_Not_", the first character is "_" — this would end up being included as "_" and uppercased → "_". This breaks the underscore test. Previously I was filtering with is_alphabetic. Now I need to handle that. Let me restructure: iterate over the characters, and track whether we've already taken the first alphabetic character of the word.

A simpler approach:

```rust
.flat_map(|word| {
    let mut first = true;
    let mut prev_lower = false;
    word.chars().filter_map(move |c| {
        let take = if first {
            first = false;
            c.is_alphabetic()
        } else {
            c.is_uppercase() && prev_lower
        };
        prev_lower = c.is_lowercase();
        take.then_some(c)
    })
})
```

Hmm, but the prev_lower update: in the case of "HyperText", H (first, alphabetic → take), p is lowercase so prev_lower=true, e, r, T is uppercase and prev_lower → take, then prev_lower=false (T is not lowercase), M is uppercase and prev_lower is false → don't take. Good.

In the case of "_Not_": _ first, not alphabetic → don't take, prev_lower=false; N is uppercase and prev_lower is false → don't take?? This fails — "The Road _Not_ Taken" expects TRNT, so the N from "_Not_" is needed. Hmm.

Wait, the expected value is "TRNT" — T, R, N, T. So "_Not_" → N. In other words, the first alphabetic character of the word counts, not the first character. Let me rethink: the rule is that the first alphabetic character of a word is taken, plus any uppercase character that follows a lowercase character.

```rust
.flat_map(|word| {
    let mut seen_alpha = false;
    let mut prev_lower = false;
    word.chars().filter_map(move |c| {
        let take = if !seen_alpha {
            c.is_alphabetic()
        } else {
            c.is_uppercase() && prev_lower
        };
        if c.is_alphabetic() {
            seen_alpha = true;
            prev_lower = c.is_lowercase();
        }
        take.then_some(c)
    })
})
```

Check "GNU": G is alphabetic, take, prev_lower=false. N is uppercase and prev_lower is false → don't take. Good.
"HyperText": H is taken, prev_lower=false. p → prev_lower=true. e, r. T is uppercase and prev_lower → take. Good.
"_Not_": _ is not alphabetic, don't take, seen_alpha is false. N is alphabetic, !seen_alpha → take. Good.
"Complementary metal-oxide semiconductor": C is taken; rest is lowercase. m is taken, o is taken, s is taken. CMOS. Good.
"Thank George It's Friday!": T, G, I, F. Good.
"First In, First Out": F, I, F, O. Good.

Let me rewrite the whole function cleanly.

